--TEST--
`iterable` and an intersection are declarable in both positions and compile to a representation — `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/nullable-union-single-value-and-enum-case-types
// `rule:types/grammar`'s grammar spells both, and until this landed a program that
// merely *declared* one type-checked and then panicked `nvs-ir` below the
// checker that accepted it. Neither is inhabitable yet — `nvs_types::expr::assign`
// has no arm making an `array<T>`, a generator or a class assignable to either
// — so this case pins the declaration, which is exactly what the arms are for.
interface Other {
    public function other(): int;
}

final class Marker implements Other {
    public function other(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

final class Declares {
    // `iterable` is `array|Traversable`: two runtime shapes, so it erases to
    // the tagged representation rather than to an object pointer.
    public static function takesIterable(iterable $p): void {}

    public static function givesIterable(): iterable {
        throw new LogicError("unreached");
    }

    // An intersection folds its members the way a union does — both of these
    // are one object pointer, so `Marker&Other` is one too.
    public static function takesBoth(Marker&Other $p): void {}

    public static function givesBoth(): Marker&Other {
        throw new LogicError("unreached");
    }
}

// A class whose methods are never called still compiles, which is the claim.
Marker $m = new Marker();
echo $m->other(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
1
